{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 25, "model_name": "abacusai/dracarys-llama-3.1-70b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187. He was the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. Raymond III was a member of the House of Toulouse and played a significant role in the politics of the Crusader states during the 12th century.\n\n*Early Life and Succession*\nRaymond III was born around 1140. His father Raymond II was the Count of Tripoli and his mother Hodierna was the daughter of King Baldwin II of Jerusalem. Raymond III succeeded his father as the Count of Tripoli in 1152 after his father's assassination. He was only around 12 years old at the time and his mother Hodierna acted as regent until he came of age.\n\nRaymond III's early reign was marked by conflicts with the neighboring Muslim states. He was forced to pay tribute to the Fatimid Caliphate and later to the Zengid dynasty. In 1164 Raymond III was captured by the Zengids while on a campaign against the Fatimids. He was held captive for several years before being released in 1165.\n\n*The Battle of Hattin*\nIn 1187 Raymond III played a significant role in the Battle of Hattin. He was one of the leaders of the Crusader army that was defeated by the forces of Saladin. The battle was a major turning point in the history of the Crusader states and led to the loss of Jerusalem and other territories.\n\n*Later Life and Legacy*\nAfter the Battle of Hattin Raymond III retreated to Tripoli where he died in 1187. He was succeeded by his nephew Bohemond IV. Raymond III's legacy is complex and multifaceted. He is remembered as a skilled military leader who fought against the Muslim states but also as a leader who was ultimately defeated and failed to protect the Crusader states.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.004134069196879864}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -6.318112355074845e-06}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -